While working on `sycl `plugin migration to `xgboost 2.0.0,` I faced the dispatching problem. In many cases we reuse `cpu `code for `sycl `devices. But in the current realization dispatching work as follow:
```
if (cpu) {
 do_on_cpu();
} else {
 do_on_cuda();
}
```
as far as `sycl `devices are not `cpu`, we fall to the `cuda `branches instead of `cpu `ones.

Current PR simply rearrange the if-else statements in the following way:
```
if (cuda) {
 do_on_cuda();
} else {
 do_on_cpu();
}
```
Such change doesn't affect any `cpu/cuda` logic, but is helpful for `sycl` development.

Use generics to allow the user to define their own request context type

```swift
struct MyContext: HBRequestContext {
    init(applicationContext: HBApplicationContext, channel: Channel, logger: Logger) {
        self.coreContext = .init(
            applicationContext: applicationContext, 
            channel: channel, 
            logger: logger
        )
        self.additionalData = nil
    }

    /// Field required by HBRequestContext
    public let coreContext: HBCoreRequestContext
    /// my extra data
    var additionalData: String?
}

public struct MiddlewareEditingAdditionalData: HBMiddleware {
    public typealias Context = MyContext

    public func apply(to request: HBRequest, context: Context, next: any HBResponder<Context>) -> EventLoopFuture<HBResponse> {
        var context = context
        context.additionalData = request.uri.queryParameters["test"]
        return next.respond(to: request, context: context)
    }
}

let app = HBApplicationBuilder(requestContext: MyContext.self)
app.middleware.add(MiddlewareEditingAdditionalData())
app.router.get("/test") { _, context in
    context.additionalData
}
try await app.buildAndRun()
```

This code must have been used in debugging a long time ago. Otherwise `return 250.;` can't be really the general value that would fit all usages. 

```cpp
  // calculation of GA errors
  template<typename T>
  double calc_error_bio(const vector<T>& v, double bio_mean, double bio_sd)
  {
    if (v.size() != 0) {
      double error = 0.;
      for (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {
        error += fabs(v[i] - bio_mean);
      }
      return error / bio_sd / v.size();
    } else {
      return 250.;
    }
  }

```

